    {
      "text": "So forward deployed engineers can now not just talk to customers, not just prototype, but actually build end-to-end solutions with coding agents.",
      "timestamp_sec": 754,
      "why": "States what changes about the role once code is cheap."
    },
    {
      "text": "But I think something more interesting is happening that we're seeing at Sierra, which is actually the lines are blurring. So product engineering is also becoming more client-facing.",
      "timestamp_sec": 754,
      "why": "The convergence claim, sourced to observation at Sierra."
    },
    {
      "text": "We think that you should pay a software platform for the value that that software delivers.",
      "timestamp_sec": 839,
      "why": "Sierra's pricing position stated plainly, and a point others contest."
    },
    {
      "text": "And we think basically that most of pricing in this market will move to outcome-based.",
      "timestamp_sec": 880,
      "why": "A falsifiable market prediction."
    },
    {
      "text": "if you have both of these things, you have forward-deployed engineers that can now contribute to the product, you have more outcome-based pricing, how do you actually guarantee the outcome? And that really is forward-deployed engineering.",
      "timestamp_sec": 880,
      "why": "Ties the two trend lines into the talk's conclusion."
    },
    {
      "text": "really in some ways everything is forward deployed engineering, or at least everything is trending that way. Product engineering, agent engineering, AI engineering, solutions engineering, and customer engineering.",
      "timestamp_sec": 918,
      "why": "The maximal version of the convergence thesis."
    },
    {
      "text": "forward deployed engineering is dead. And long live forward deployed engineering.",
      "timestamp_sec": 958,
      "why": "The closing formulation of the paradox."
    }
